#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091946 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1316" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1317" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1318" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\nII. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092159 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9981" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5582da46-e860-5de6-9a80-bf70a2d5dbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435031} {:answer/id #uuid "5eba3768-372e-57d2-9f12-5041b396542f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435032} {:answer/id #uuid "8831a439-f885-54bc-913a-726e2783d364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435036} {:answer/id #uuid "d08c9c2d-c678-54bf-8b79-6e361af4291c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435040} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c7fe9a-33a1-5066-a984-2b33bf4beec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8831a439-f885-54bc-913a-726e2783d364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435036} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d2fe7da-7162-512f-acab-d05c2d961b5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2357828d-1c68-5412-b5ab-9d7c9a1f8663" :question/math-academy-id "q-111386" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $8,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8$\n\nII. $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $32$\n\nIII. $\\frac{Q}{P}$ is a polynomial of degree $4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}$ and ${x}^{8},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{8}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q,$ still contains ${x}^{8}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $8.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}$ and ${x}^{8},$ respectively, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{4+8}={x}^{12},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $12.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}$ and $Q={x}^{8}+1.$ Then, $\\frac{Q}{P}=\\frac{{x}^{8}+1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045d0a65-b7d8-5bfb-9b43-906b5283f84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435023} {:answer/id #uuid "14893a56-3116-5bfd-b0d1-990957ad1998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435026} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8cb1de-ccb8-5910-9ec5-4dba43048b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435030} {:answer/id #uuid "79f71a19-7850-565d-91b5-d77d8197d35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435034} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dd91c3-ef1a-5e41-ab0d-dd262ea8b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4dd91c3-ef1a-5e41-ab0d-dd262ea8b55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435039} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b7845b-8ad3-57c0-a6ae-28437acfe3a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44852747-843f-5a59-aaa4-c3f273f2d780" :question/math-academy-id "q-111389" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n\nII. $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $2$\n\nIII. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+x$ and $Q=-{x}^{3}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{3}+x-{x}^{3}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}={x}^{2},$ which is a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=1+{x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{1+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+1,$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} {:answer/id #uuid "46da9874-30bc-583c-a863-5aae9560a9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269913} {:answer/id #uuid "59a1700f-ccf6-5e35-9e43-a7880fc832ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270065} {:answer/id #uuid "75df7d2e-780f-56cd-92ad-5720fda4a706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270310} {:answer/id #uuid "c456bd0a-afad-5d98-8f69-257b595ed130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce55ae1-51e7-5113-8b9b-9cc54aa7d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269380} :answer-field/id #uuid "903f2292-1c52-539d-84aa-b1a6a93b0960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6faa06e9-6d50-5778-8c1a-50c6b3712803" :question/math-academy-id "q-141234" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(x)+(-{x}^{3}-1)$\n\nII. $(2{x}^{4}-3)÷({x}^{2})$\n\nIII. $({x}^{2})-(2{x}^{5}+4x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22efd206-8495-5c5b-82c3-8b22f6169935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435028} {:answer/id #uuid "5f573ec0-e420-5d5a-bf15-12b9f19228c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435033} {:answer/id #uuid "972d63cb-deb1-504d-983c-3afd89efb163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435037} {:answer/id #uuid "db22347e-ef69-54bb-bdf3-e3c186808327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435041} {:answer/id #uuid "e1cafff7-1db2-590c-918b-31d40bb1ec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22efd206-8495-5c5b-82c3-8b22f6169935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435028} :answer-field/id #uuid "3642f741-bfcb-537b-8055-8377c2f4a23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17b4b8a5-2a1a-5b8f-9d66-afed9881e7a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-141235" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(6-{x}^{3})-({x}^{2}+4)$\n\nII. $(3{x}^{3}+x)\\cdot (2{x}^{4}+4)$\n\nIII. $(1-3{x}^{3})÷(3x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression II gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3{x}^{3}}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3x}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea4ef8b-f443-5c71-a66d-968c904fd206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435024} {:answer/id #uuid "13fc7668-35a7-52b2-8d6b-1ef66cb2dee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435025} {:answer/id #uuid "2219b244-05db-51b1-afdc-21618a8ff9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435027} {:answer/id #uuid "24ca3551-0275-501a-8224-d0c29cf4662c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435029} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa2f6a3-912d-5f28-9899-584bb9cb2f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea4ef8b-f443-5c71-a66d-968c904fd206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435024} :answer-field/id #uuid "18301aa5-8122-537f-afa8-0033da27bf6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8719458-2bd9-576f-821e-1af82c528d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141256" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $9$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{3+9}={x}^{12}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0cbcf02-f7a2-529e-aa13-2e2ba426321d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0cbcf02-f7a2-529e-aa13-2e2ba426321d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435038} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad249e65-93ce-5e24-9e7f-3d95d55441e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7a2bab2-b83f-574b-87f1-a6024a7e2d15" :question/math-academy-id "q-269923" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $12$,  the highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $12.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{12}$ and $Q={x}^{12}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{12}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{12},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f727d8b3-fde4-50e2-8609-459d27c079b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f727d8b3-fde4-50e2-8609-459d27c079b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435044} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1af39d-b0cd-5234-a9e5-f7a9cf9d0cfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68e7c276-c403-5f7f-bb19-14fc56277444" :question/math-academy-id "q-277897" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$ and $8$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $7,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{7}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{8}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{8}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{7}\\cdot {x}^{8}={x}^{7+8}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8782acb7-46a5-5731-bad8-7ee07d94b5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8782acb7-46a5-5731-bad8-7ee07d94b5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435035} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f01642-3348-59ae-92b2-38fd784b6dae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55ca9374-3367-5974-bb86-994acf801013" :question/math-academy-id "q-277912" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $7$, the **smallest possible** degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P=9{x}^{7}$ and $Q=-9{x}^{7}-4.$ Then $P+Q=-4,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$"}]}
